{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>I will appease his face with the gift</b> — I will nullify his wrath. So too: \"and your covenant with death will be annulled\" (Isaiah 28:18), \"you will not be able to annul it\" (ibid. 47:11). And it appears to me that every כפרה that is said concerning sin and transgression and that which is said concerning faces — all of them are in the language of wiping away and removing. And in the Aramaic tongue this is so in many places: כפרי ידה (she wipes her hands) (Gittin 56a), and also in the language of Scripture the sprinkling-bowls are called כפורי זהב (Ezra 1:10), because with them one wipes one's hands on the rim of the bowl — this is the language of our Master Solomon. So Onkelos too rendered (Onkelos on Genesis 32:21): אנינה לרגזה (I will nullify his wrath). If so, the meaning would be that Jacob thought in his heart, \"I will appease his face,\" and Scripture informs us of this, for it is not fitting that the messengers themselves would say such a thing, and thus Rabbi Abraham explained. But it is not right in my eyes that Scripture would need to inform us now of this thought of his, when it is known to all who send a gift. Moreover, if that were so, it would have been fitting for Scripture to mention this at the beginning; and behold, it says גם הוא אחרינו כי חשב אכפרה פניו, for now it adds nothing to that earlier instruction. Rather, the correct interpretation is that now he added and specified to them that they should say, \"Behold, he too is behind us,\" in language of honor — in the manner of: \"Behold, your servant Jacob is behind us.\" And: \"He has preceded us, to give the ransom of his life upon seeing the face of your honor with this gift\" — just as servants give their ransom when they are granted permission to see the king's face — \"and afterward I will see his face, for perhaps he will lift my face and honor me to be among those who see the king's face.\" And this is a course of elevation proceeding from his fear of him. And אכפרה פניו comes in the manner of \"and a wise man will appease it\" (Proverbs 16:14) — he gives a ransom against the wrath. And the language of wiping in the sense of כפור is not the sacred tongue but the Aramaic tongue. And likewise כפורי זהב is the name of the sprinkling-bowls in Babylonia, for כפרה never applies to sin, but rather one says לכפר על נפשתיכם (Exodus 30:15), לכפר עליו ונסלח לו (Numbers 15:28) — concerning his life, and one says אכפרה בעד חטאתכם (Exodus 32:30), and all of them are from the language of ונתנו איש כפר נפשו (ibid. 30:12), which is redemption."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {0 "In the phrase והנה גם הוא אחרינו כי חשב אכפרה פניו, Ramban appears to quote the verse text itself (Genesis 32:20–21) rather than offering a paraphrase; the translation renders the Hebrew words as they stand in the biblical verse."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:39:21.998104172Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}